.IX Title "ASN1_BIT_STRING_GET_LENGTH 3ossl"
.TH ASN1_BIT_STRING_GET_LENGTH 3ossl "2026-04-14" "4.0.0" "OpenSSL"
.\" For nroff, turn off justification.  Always turn off hyphenation; it makes
.\" way too many mistakes in technical documents.
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH "NAME"
ASN1_BIT_STRING_set1,
ASN1_BIT_STRING_get_length \- ASN1_BIT_STRING accessors
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 1
\&  #include <openssl/asn1.h>
\&
\&  int ASN1_BIT_STRING_get_length(const ASN1_BIT_STRING *bitstr, size_t *length, int *unused_bits);
\&  int ASN1_BIT_STRING_set1(ASN1_BIT_STRING *bitstr, const uint8_t *data, size_t length, int unused_bits);
.Ve
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
The \s-1ASN.1 BIT STRING\s0 type holds a bit string of arbitrary bit length.
In the distinguished encoding rules \s-1DER,\s0 its bits are encoded in
groups of eight, leaving between zero and seven bits of the
last octet unused. If there are unused bits, they must all be set to zero.
.PP
\&\fIASN1_BIT_STRING_get_length()\fR returns the number of octets in \fIbitstr\fR
containing bit values in \fIlength\fR and the number of unused bits in
the last octet in \fIunused_bits\fR. The value returned in
\&\fIunused_bits\fR is guaranteed to be between 0 and 7, inclusive.
.PP
\&\fIASN1_BIT_STRING_set1()\fR sets the type of \fIbitstr\fR to
\&\fIV_ASN1_BIT_STRING\fR and its octets to the bits in the byte string
\&\fIdata\fR of length \fIlength\fR octets, making sure that the last
\&\fIunused_bits\fR bits in the last byte are zero.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
.IX Header "RETURN VALUES"
\&\fIASN1_BIT_STRING_get_length()\fR returns 1 on success or 0 if the encoding
of \fIbitstr\fR is internally inconsistent, or if one of \fIbitstr\fR,
\&\fIlength\fR, or \fIunused_bits\fR is \s-1NULL.\s0
.PP
\&\fIASN1_BIT_STRING_set1()\fR returns 1 on success or 0 if memory allocation
fails or if \fIbitstr\fR is \s-1NULL , \s0\fIlength\fR is too large, \fIlength\fRis
zero and \fIunused_bits\fR is nonzero, \fIunused_bits\fR is less than 0 or
greater than 7, or any unused bit in the last octet of \fIdata\fR is
nonzero.
